The core user interface (UI) flow of a standard Unified Payments Interface (UPI) payment app—such as Google Pay, PhonePe, or Paytm—is optimized for speed, security, and minimal friction.
The architecture is divided into three distinct phases: Onboarding, The Payment Journey, and Post-Transaction Tracking.

1. Onboarding & Registration Flow

This is a one-time sequence required to bind the user's device and bank account to the UPI network.
[App Launch] ➔ [Mobile Number Input] ➔ [SIM Verification (SMS)] 
                     ➔ [Set App Lock] ➔ [Bank Selection] ➔ [Fetch & Link Account]
• Device Binding: The app automatically sends a background SMS from the user's phone to verify that the mobile number matches the device hardware.
• Bank Account Fetching: The app queries the National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI) network using the verified mobile number to securely retrieve all linked bank accounts.
• UPI PIN Creation: If a UPI PIN isn't already set for the bank account, the user inputs their debit card details (last 6 digits and expiry) and verifies it via a bank OTP to create a 4 or 6-digit secure UPI PIN.

2. The Core Payment Flows

UPI apps generally split transactions into three main entry points based on how the user interacts with the recipient.

Flow A: Scan & Pay (QR Code)

This is the most common retail merchant flow.
1. Entry: Tap the "Scan QR" icon on the home screen.
2. Camera Interface: The system requests camera permissions and opens a viewfinder.
3. Parsing: The app decodes the QR code string to extract the merchant's UPI ID (VPA), name, and transaction amount (if it's a dynamic QR).
4. Amount Screen: The user enters the payment amount (if not preset) and an optional note.
5. Account Selection: A bottom sheet displays the default bank account with an option to switch accounts.
6. Security Overlay: The native, secure NPCI overlay appears. The user types their UPI PIN.

Flow B: Pay to Contact / Phone Number

Used for Peer-to-Peer (P2P) transfers.
1. Entry: Tap "Pay Contacts" or "To Mobile Number".
2. Contact List: The app fetches the phone book or provides a search bar.
3. Recipient Validation: Once a number is entered, the app checks the UPI database to verify if the contact is registered and displays the legal name linked to the account to prevent misdirected payments.
4. Chat/Payment Screen: Opens a chat-like interface displaying previous transaction history with a prominent "Pay" button.
5. Execution: Amount Entry ➔ Bank Selection ➔ UPI PIN Screen.

Flow C: Pay to UPI ID / VPA

Used when transferring money without a phone number or QR code.
1. Entry: Tap "Pay to UPI ID".
2. Input: User types the Virtual Payment Address (e.g., username@bank).
3. Verification: The user clicks "Verify". The system queries the UPI switch and surfaces the beneficiary's registered account name.
4. Execution: Amount Entry ➔ Bank Selection ➔ UPI PIN Screen.

3. Post-Transaction & Status Flow

Once the UPI PIN is submitted, the app transitions into an asynchronous polling state while communicating with the banking servers.
[Processing Screen (Loader)] ➔ [Status Screen (Success / Fail / Pending)] 
                                      ➔ [Receipt Generation & Notification]
• Processing Screen: A non-interruptible loading screen appears. Users are explicitly warned not to press back or close the app.
• The Success Screen:
	• Features a distinct green checkmark animation.
	• Plays an audible confirmation sound (highly critical for merchant reassurance).
	• Displays the Transaction ID, Bank Ref Number, Amount, and Recipient Name.
• Exception Handling (Failures): If the payment fails (e.g., wrong PIN, bank server downtime, insufficient funds), the UI surfaces a red warning card accompanied by a contextual call-to-action like "Try Again" or "Switch Bank Account".
